      "title": "Lottomatica to absorb CIRSA in all-share merger creating a EUR 2bn EBITDA group across Italy and Spain",
      "text": "Lottomatica and CIRSA agreed an all-share EU cross-border merger on 2 September, and Lottomatica CEO Guglielmo Angelozzi spent the 3 September investor call insisting it is a 'low-risk proposition'. The arithmetic: CIRSA valued at roughly 6x expected 2026 EBITDA of EUR 800m-EUR 820m, an exchange ratio of 0.668 new Lottomatica shares per CIRSA share, Lottomatica holders at 67.5% and CIRSA holders at 32.5%, and pro forma adjusted EBITDA of about EUR 2bn - second only to Flutter among listed operators. Blackstone, CIRSA's majority owner, emerges as the largest single shareholder at around 24% with two board seats. What makes this more than a scale story is where the EBITDA sits. Online sports betting and gaming is already the largest vertical at 48% of combined H1 pro forma adjusted EBITDA, distributed gaming is 27% and casinos 25%, with roughly 80% of EBITDA concentrated in Italy and Spain and 97% coming from markets where the group holds the number one position. Angelozzi's stated rationale is explicitly not a turnaround: he told investors previous cross-border deals failed because Lottomatica 'tried to make too many changes to an asset', and that 'you get no additional risk, and you get the benefits of the new markets and the online opportunities on top of the synergies'. Read as a technology signal, that is a commitment to leave CIRSA's retail estate largely alone - which means the EUR 115m of annual pre-tax cash synergies targeted by year three has to come from opex, interest and duplicated back-office systems rather than a platform migration. The commercial consequence for suppliers is immediate: CIRSA has completed more than 130 acquisitions since 2015 and arrives with a heterogeneous estate spanning Spain, Italy, Colombia, Panama, Peru, Mexico and Morocco, plus an estimated 6% share of a fragmented Spanish online market that Lottomatica intends to grow using CIRSA's retail platform as the acquisition channel. That is a build-or-buy decision on omnichannel tech taken by a group that will, from Q2 2027, be the dominant single buyer of platform, wallet and CRM services in southern Europe. Anyone holding contracts inside either estate should expect a consolidation review well before completion, and Italian antitrust is the one procedural risk worth tracking - Angelozzi says concentration 'will still be below 40% in each relevant market'.",
