      "title": "Bally's Intralot carries EUR 1.6bn adjusted net debt into the evoke integration as completion slips towards Q1 2027",
      "text": "Bally's Intralot's H1 2026 results put adjusted net debt above EUR 1.6bn, roughly EUR 135m higher year on year, while it presses ahead with the takeover of William Hill and Mr Green parent evoke - which separately carries GBP 1.89bn of debt. The trading picture is genuinely fine: group H1 revenue of EUR 544.2m against EUR 182m a year earlier, adjusted EBITDA up from EUR 60.2m to EUR 184.8m, Bally's International Interactive contributing EUR 377.6m of revenue and EUR 132.8m of EBITDA, UK constant-currency growth of 11.6% in Q2 and a record NGR quarter. But the group still posted a EUR 7.2m H1 loss against a EUR 9.8m profit a year earlier, and the debt build has identifiable causes rather than trading ones: an EUR 86m payment for its Victoria electronic gaming machine licence, EUR 20.5m from investing activities, EUR 20.8m of treasury share transactions and EUR 14.5m of transaction fees and bond issuance costs, layered on top of a GBP 400m institutional loan, a GBP 200m amortising facility and a GBP 261.7m senior note facility raised in July specifically for the evoke deal. CEO Robeson Reeves has described evoke's debt as 'non-recourse' to Bally's Intralot, and confirmed the group 'continue[s] to expect the scheme to become effective in the fourth quarter of 2026 or the first quarter of 2027'. Non-recourse is a balance-sheet distinction, not an operational one. The combined entity will own William Hill, 888, Mr Green, Jackpotjoy, Virgin Games, Monopoly Casino UK, Rainbow Riches Casino and Bally Bet in a single UK portfolio, running two platform estates with overlapping player bases, divergent compliance histories and a 40% UK remote gaming duty environment to absorb. That is one of the harder multi-brand PAM, wallet and CRM consolidation problems in the market, and it will be attempted by a group whose interest bill is rising. evoke shareholders approved the deal by 99% on 17 August; other regulatory approvals remain outstanding. For suppliers into either estate the practical work is unglamorous and time-sensitive: check change-of-control and termination-for-insolvency wording, confirm payment terms, and assume that discretionary technology spend is the first line reviewed while the financing is unresolved. The platform rationalisation decision itself will be taken well before completion.",
