      "title": "New Jersey asks the Supreme Court to resolve the Kalshi circuit split after the Ninth Circuit sides with Nevada",
      "text": "New Jersey filed a petition for certiorari on 2 September in KalshiEX v. Flaherty, asking the US Supreme Court to decide whether the CFTC's authority over event contracts preempts state gambling law. The filing follows a clean and unusually explicit appellate conflict. In April the Third Circuit held that Kalshi's sports event contracts are swaps traded on a CFTC-registered designated contract market and that the Commodity Exchange Act likely preempted New Jersey gambling law, upholding an injunction letting Kalshi operate unlicensed. On 28 August the Ninth Circuit ruled for Nevada on the opposite reasoning: the contracts are sports bets, treating game outcomes as swaps would federalise sports betting without clear direction from Congress, and CFTC Rule 40.11 bars registered exchanges from listing contracts involving gaming. New Jersey did not seek en banc rehearing in the Third Circuit, instead taking extensions from Justice Samuel Alito that moved its deadline to 3 September. Attorney General Jennifer Davenport framed it directly: 'Companies like Kalshi claim to offer legal sports betting in all 50 States, but they refuse to follow the gambling laws of any State... Congress did not silently make the sports-betting industry immune from state law.' The court is not obliged to grant certiorari, but a square circuit split materially raises the odds. The reason this is the most consequential open question in North American product planning is cost symmetry. Licensed operators carry state licensing, tax, geofencing, KYC and responsible gambling obligations that CFTC-regulated exchanges currently do not, and that asymmetry is now being priced into roadmaps on both sides. A Kalshi win effectively creates a fifty-state distribution channel that needs exchange-style order matching, clearing and RFQ pricing rather than sportsbook odds compilation; a state win collapses much of the event-contract build-out of the past 18 months. Note also that the Ninth Circuit hung its reasoning on Rule 40.11, which puts the CFTC's own rulebook rather than state gambling statutes at the centre of the fight - and gives the Commission a route to change the answer without waiting for the courts. Canada, meanwhile, has already drawn the line, with its securities and investment regulators ruling out sports event contracts entirely on 27 August.",
