      "title": "Lula says he is 'two thirds' decided on Brazilian online betting, with the SPA absent from the room",
      "text": "President Lula convened ministers and civil society representatives on 1 September to debate online betting, described it as an 'evil for society', and closed by saying he had 'two thirds of the decision made' without specifying what the decision is. The most important detail is who was not there: the Secretariat of Prizes and Betting, the Ministry of Finance body that actually regulates and supervises the sector, along with operator association ANJL and the Instituto Brasileiro de Jogo Responsavel. A debate about prohibiting a licensed market, conducted without the technical regulator or the licensees present, is a materially different risk signal than a consultation. Casa Civil chief minister Miriam Belchior conceded the point herself: 'We have been busy regulating and supervising, but this is not proving enough.' Health Minister Alexandre Padilha reframed gambling as a public health matter, citing a professional training course whose 20,000 places were oversubscribed and psychological care capacity rising from around 600 consultations a month to 100,000, with contracting under way for up to 2 million. Sport Minister Paulo Henrique Cordeiro acknowledged roughly BRL 500m (USD 96.6m) reached sport in the first half but said 'it is difficult to effectively defend this economic activity in the country'. CNC's Leandro Domingues Teixeira called a ban an 'extreme but necessary decision'. Two operational numbers came out of the session that matter regardless of the political outcome. First, 1.2 million people have now requested self-exclusion via the federal platform, which raises the stakes considerably on centralised exclusion-register checks at registration, login and deposit - a real-time integration, not a nightly batch. Second, 37 fintechs have been notified over payment flows linked to illegal betting, with Ministry of Finance and Federal Police investigations pointing at schemes potentially moving up to BRL 50bn, and Apple and Google contacted over clandestine apps. That pulls PSPs and wallet partners directly inside the enforcement perimeter, which means cashier and payment-orchestration choices now need documented rationale rather than commercial justification alone. No decision date has been set; the call rests with the president after internal government debate.",
