      "title": "Fanatics ships a single geo-aware app spanning sportsbook, casino and event contracts on one FanCash balance",
      "text": "Fanatics launched Fanatics Sports & Casino on 2 September, ahead of the NFL season, collapsing three previously separate products into one login and one FanCash balance: sportsbook across 23 states plus Washington DC, online casino in Michigan, New Jersey, Pennsylvania and West Virginia, and Fanatics Markets event contracts in 22 states and four US territories. The app detects location and adjusts which verticals a customer can even see, so the product surface changes state by state. CEO Matt King: 'Fanatics Sports & Casino gives fans across America one app where the experience travels with them, from betting the game, to trading on its biggest moments, to playing casino games' - adding that 'every one of those bets, trades or plays earns FanCash'. It follows DraftKings' Super App in March and Betr's Polymarket-partnered stack, making this the third genuine US multi-vertical super app and confirming the pattern as table stakes rather than differentiation. The front end is the easy half. Underneath, this requires one KYC and account record, a single balance that has to be legally partitioned by vertical and by jurisdiction, and an entitlement engine that resolves at session level which products a given user may access - with three different regulatory regimes and three different margin profiles behind them. The loyalty layer is the hardest part and the clearest tell: FanCash spanning betting, casino and CFTC-regulated event contracts implies a unified player-value model across products whose economics and permissible marketing differ fundamentally, redeemable against merchandise, tickets and collectibles through the Fanatics ONE tier structure. Fanatics also acquired Water Street Labs in July so that Fanatics Markets owns its own exchange and clearinghouse, meaning it controls the full stack rather than reselling someone else's venue. For B2B platform vendors the procurement question crystallises here: can a PAM express product entitlement, jurisdictional partitioning and cross-vertical bonus wallets as configuration rather than code? If not, operators will build it themselves - and entitlement-by-geolocation should be expected in the next round of North American RFPs.",
