    {
      "title": "Argue about the harm metric now, because it is going into your risk engine either way",
      "text": "BOS has put a precise, implementable objection to Spelinspektionen's draft technical requirements, and every line of it lands in the risk engine rather than the policy binder. The regulator proposes using the difference between a player's stakes and winnings as a financial harm indicator; BOS argues that double-counts recycled winnings and proposes deposits and withdrawals as the primary marker, with stakes and winnings kept as a supplementary play-intensity signal. That is the right hill to fight on, because net deposits and withdrawals is already the ground truth most PAM data models use for affordability, whereas turnover-based signals generate false positives at volume and swamp intervention teams. Two further provisions are worse operationally than they look. Treating play between 00:00 and 06:00 as a harm marker - which BOS calls 'arbitrary' given international sports schedules - would flag legitimate overnight sports betting as high-risk by default. And mandatory account suspension where a licensee cannot reliably confirm a player received feedback on harmful play converts a message-delivery problem into a revenue-stopping event, which means read receipts, in-product acknowledgement and fallback channels stop being nice-to-have and become critical infrastructure; BOS proposes passive confirmation instead, verifying the communication was seen or tracking whether activity improved. The proposed cut in the inactivity logout timer from two hours to one degrades in-play UX and, as BOS notes, fragments the session data used for risk monitoring in the first place. Hoffstedt's closing standard is the one to hold any draft rule against: 'In order for the regulations to have the intended effect, they need to be clear, proportionate, technology-neutral and practically feasible.' If you hold a Swedish licence, model the false-positive and intervention-volume impact of each provision before the rules land, not after.",
      "source": "https://sbcnews.co.uk/europe/2026/09/01/spelinspektionen-update/",
      "sourceLabel": "SBC News - BOS letter"
    },
    {
      "title": "Your exclusion suppression probably does not cover the VIP account manager's phone",
      "text": "Colorado fined Fanatics $20,000 for a failure worth studying in detail because it is architectural rather than negligent. A customer self-excluded for five years on 15 January 2026. A VIP team member texted him a promotional offer on 1 February. Fanatics identified the problem on 4 February, escalated to VIP leadership and distributed training materials to customer-facing staff. A VIP team member texted the same self-excluded customer another promotional offer on 17 February. Training did not fix it, because training was never the control. The pattern is one of the most common gaps in operator CRM architecture: automated campaign suppression works cleanly against the exclusion register, and then breaks the moment a human account manager sends one-to-one outreach from a separate tool that never checks it. The fix is not a policy or a briefing - exclusion status has to be a hard gate at the messaging layer that every outbound path traverses, including manual sends, VIP channels and third-party tools. The remediation Colorado imposed makes the auditability requirement explicit: Fanatics must audit its self-exclusion list for comparable contacts across the whole period from 1 January 2024 to 1 March 2026 and file a detailed report. If you cannot reconstruct two years of outbound contact by exclusion status on demand, you do not have the logs the regulator will ask for. Colorado is also becoming the strictest configuration profile in the US - SB26-131, signed in June, bans credit card funding, caps bettors at six deposits per day and prohibits marketing terms including 'bonus bet' and 'no sweat' - so treat it as the state that sets your suppression and cashier requirements rather than the exception you handle last.",
      "source": "https://sbcamericas.com/2026/09/01/colorado-fines-fanatics-sportsbook/",
      "sourceLabel": "SBC Americas"
    },
    {
      "title": "France has written down what your fraud stack must be able to prove in court",
      "text": "ANJ's new player-fraud guide, published 31 August after consultation with operators and the gaming ombudsman, is more operationally useful than a new prohibition because it specifies evidence rather than conduct. It creates no new legal obligations, and that is the point: it clarifies when operators may close accounts, withhold or return balances, cancel bets or redistribute tournament prizes across six fraud categories - identity fraud, payment fraud and abusive chargebacks, in-game cheating, money-dumping and sporting event manipulation - and, critically, sets out the 'faisceaux d'indices', the converging indicators French courts have previously accepted, including IP addresses, device logs and connection timings. The standard is proof, not suspicion. Read that as a data-retention and audit-trail specification: device fingerprints, session logs, connection timings and the reasoning behind each decision stop being incidental telemetry and become evidentiary records you may have to produce against a legal challenge. Three concrete asks follow. Reserving a balance where bank account ownership cannot be confirmed needs the account-verification trail to support it. Two-factor authentication on withdrawals and on changes to banking details, which the guide emphasises for account-takeover mitigation, is a cashier and account-security requirement many operators still treat as optional friction. And the poker collusion and bot guidance - courts have validated redistributing illegitimate winnings to affected players where robust data supports it - only works if the contractual terms and the detection logic were designed together, which in most stacks they were not. Context on the enforcement appetite: ANJ fined an unnamed operator EUR 500,000 in July 2026 for failing to correctly risk-rate 29 players, six missed entirely and 23 placed in a lower tier. For anyone entering France the checklist is explicit - rewrite the terms, harden the evidence pipeline, re-test technical safeguards against certification expectations.",
      "source": "https://igamingbusiness.com/legal-compliance/france-tighten-operator-guidance-player-fraud-courts-refine-evidentiary-standards/",
      "sourceLabel": "iGaming Business"
    },
    {
      "title": "One AML configuration across every product class, driven by behaviour not product labels",
      "text": "Betr's Head of Legal Matt Kane makes an argument that is really about platform design rather than compliance philosophy: the operator took the framework built for regulated US online sports betting and applied it unchanged to Gold/Sweeps Coins dual-currency products, daily fantasy sports and skill-based games, with prediction markets next. 'We made a conscious decision not to lower that bar just because the regulatory framework was different. The product classification may change, but a lot of the underlying risks don't.' The reusability is the commercial payoff - a single compliance configuration lets a new vertical go live without standing up a fresh controls programme - but it only works if the risk engine, transaction monitoring and KYC orchestration are genuinely product-agnostic and driven by behavioural signals rather than product-type flags. His dual-currency approach shows what that means concretely: identification and KYC at the front, then continuous monitoring of 'the velocity and pattern of purchases and redemptions, rapid cycling of funds, or activity that simply doesn't make economic or behavioral sense'. Note what he explicitly does not do, which is add onboarding friction for everyone. Moving scrutiny downstream and making it event-driven protects conversion while keeping the controls, and it is the same trade-off every affordability and AML journey is now resolving. Two other points worth carrying into a cashier review. Kane's target is 'sustainable approval' rather than the highest possible authorisation rate - maximising approvals while generating disputes means, in his framing, you have won the wrong battle, so authorisation rate in isolation is the wrong KPI. And his ask of the card schemes is 'more risk-based underwriting and less reliance on broad product labels', on the grounds that two operators in the same category can carry entirely different risk profiles. His summary line is the one to steal: 'an emerging game format shouldn't mean an emerging compliance program.'",
      "source": "https://paymentexpert.com/2026/09/01/betr-matt-kane-sbc-summit-interview/",
      "sourceLabel": "Payment Expert interview"
    }
