    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "Bally's Intralot carries EUR 1.6bn adjusted net debt into evoke integration as close slips towards Q1 2027",
    "meta": "SBC News | 1 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Bally's Intralot reported H1 2026 adjusted net debt of more than EUR 1.6bn, up around EUR 135m year on year, as it presses ahead with its takeover of William Hill and Mr Green parent evoke. H1 revenue reached EUR 544.2m with adjusted EBITDA of EUR 184.8m, up from EUR 60.2m, but the group posted a EUR 7.2m loss versus a EUR 9.8m profit a year earlier. evoke separately carries GBP 1.89bn of debt and CEO Robeson Reeves expects the scheme to become effective in Q4 2026 or Q1 2027.",
    "why": "Combined leverage of this scale sets the integration budget before a single migration decision is made, and history says the technology roadmap is what gets cut first when interest costs bite. The group will end up owning William Hill, 888, Mr Green, Jackpotjoy, Virgin Games, Monopoly Casino UK, Rainbow Riches Casino and Bally Bet in one UK portfolio, which is a genuinely hard multi-brand PAM, wallet and CRM consolidation problem with overlapping player bases and heterogeneous compliance histories. Reeves calling evoke's debt 'non-recourse' keeps the balance sheets nominally separate but does nothing to reduce the operational complexity of running two platform estates in a 40% UK remote duty environment. The 11.6% Q2 UK constant currency growth and all-time high NGR claim suggest the interactive assets are performing, so the integration risk is self-inflicted rather than demand-driven. Suppliers into either estate should be planning for a consolidation review in the first half of 2027.",
    "expanded": "Publishing H1 2026 results in the week to 1 September 2026, Athens Euronext-listed Bally's Intralot declared adjusted net debt above EUR 1.6bn, roughly EUR 135m higher year on year. The increase was driven largely by an EUR 86m payment relating to its electronic gaming machine licence in Victoria, Australia, plus EUR 20.5m from investing activities, net interest payments, EUR 20.8m from treasury share transactions and EUR 14.5m in transaction fees and bond issuance costs. The group was formed in July 2025 when Intralot acquired Bally's International Interactive (BII), the international B2B assets of Bally's Corporation, which subsequently became a major shareholder; Robeson Reeves, CEO of the American firm, took the same role at Bally's Intralot. BII delivered EUR 377.6m of revenue and EUR 132.8m of adjusted EBITDA in H1 2026, with no year-over-year comparatives available. Group H1 revenue was EUR 544.2m against EUR 182m a year earlier, and Q2 revenue rose from EUR 86.5m to EUR 276.1m. Adjusted EBITDA climbed from EUR 60.2m to EUR 184.8m for the half and from EUR 20m to EUR 84.7m in Q2, but the group recorded a EUR 7.2m H1 loss versus a EUR 9.8m profit in H1 2025. Financing has been layered: a six-year GBP 400m institutional loan and four-year GBP 200m amortising loan from Greek banks around the 2025 merger, then a GBP 261.7m senior note facility in July 2026 to support the evoke deal. evoke's own H1 2026 accounts show GBP 1.89bn of debt, which Reeves has previously described to SBC News as 'non-recourse' to Bally's Intralot. UK constant currency growth reached 11.6% in Q2 with record NGR, and Spain grew 9% partly on BII integration. evoke shareholders approved the deal by 99% on 17 August; other regulatory approvals remain pending. Reeves said: 'We continue to expect the scheme to become effective in the fourth quarter of 2026 or the first quarter of 2027.'",
