    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "ANJ publishes player fraud guide setting the evidentiary bar French operators must meet before voiding bets or seizing balances",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 1 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "France's ANJ has published a practical anti-fraud guide for licensed online gambling operators, released on Monday 31 August after consultation with operators and the gaming ombudsman. It covers six fraud categories - identity fraud, payment fraud and abusive chargebacks, in-game cheating, money-dumping and sporting event manipulation - and clarifies when operators may close accounts, withhold or return balances, cancel bets or redistribute tournament prizes. Critically, it sets out the 'converging indicators' French courts have accepted, including IP addresses, device logs and connection timings, and requires proof rather than suspicion.",
    "why": "This is a regulator writing down what the fraud stack has to be able to prove in court, which is far more operationally useful than a new prohibition. Voiding a bet or reserving a balance on a suspicion alone becomes legally fragile, so audit trails - device fingerprints, session logs, connection timings, decision reasoning - move from nice-to-have telemetry to evidentiary records with retention obligations. The specific push for two-factor authentication on withdrawals and on changes to banking details is a cashier and account-security requirement that many European operators still treat as optional friction. The poker collusion and bot guidance validating redistribution of illegitimate winnings only works if the contractual terms exist and the data is robust, so T&Cs and detection logic have to be designed together. For anyone entering France, the practical checklist is explicit: rewrite the terms, harden the evidence pipeline, and re-test technical safeguards against certification expectations.",
    "expanded": "iGaming Business reported on 1 September 2026 that ANJ, France's gambling regulator, had issued a practical guide to help licensed online operators identify, document and respond to player fraud. Published on the Monday after consultation with operators and the gaming ombudsman, the guide clarifies expectations around existing anti-fraud and AML rules without creating new legal obligations, while effectively tightening the operational framework operators must observe. It addresses long-standing questions about when it is reasonable to close accounts, withhold or return player balances, cancel bets, or redistribute tournament prizes deemed fraudulently obtained, and offers insight into the evidentiary standards French courts expect before corrective action that may be legally challenged. Six fraud categories are identified: identity fraud, payment fraud and abusive chargebacks, in-game cheating, money-dumping and sporting event manipulation. For identity fraud, operators are advised to close accounts once fraud is established and to reserve balances where bank account ownership cannot be confirmed. On account takeovers, the guide emphasises technical mitigations including two-factor authentication for withdrawals and for changes to banking details. For poker collusion and bot use, recommended contractual provisions allow operators to redistribute illegitimate winnings to affected players, with the guide noting courts have validated such action where supported by robust data. ANJ calls for proof rather than suspicion, setting out the 'faisceaux d'indices' or converging indicators French courts have previously accepted, including IP addresses, device logs and connection timings. Operators are urged to strengthen terms and conditions, maintain robust evidence, adopt technical standards, and review KYC and fraud detection protocols against the recommendations and certification expectations. The guide follows ANJ's July 2026 EUR 500,000 fine on an unnamed operator, 'Company X', which failed to correctly risk-rate 29 players - six missed entirely and 23 placed in a lower tier.",
    "source": "https://igamingbusiness.com/legal-compliance/france-tighten-operator-guidance-player-fraud-courts-refine-evidentiary-standards/",
