    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "Nederlandse Loterij forces Skyhills offline in the Netherlands and targets Curacao trust intermediaries",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 2 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Nederlandse Loterij has sent cease-and-desist notices to companies and directors connected to the unlicensed site Skyhills across Costa Rica, North Macedonia, the UK, Curacao, Malta and the Marshall Islands, and confirmed the platform is now inaccessible in the Netherlands. Skyhills is the third illegal operator targeted after Lalabet and Qbet, with a June court ruling in the Lalabet case ordering its operators to cease activity and disclose ownership information. The state-backed operator estimates illegal operators hold around half the Dutch online market and account for roughly 95% of online gambling adverts.",
    "why": "A licensed incumbent doing private enforcement across six jurisdictions is a notable shift - the pressure point is corporate and payment infrastructure rather than domain blocking, which is where these platforms have always been resilient. CEO Arjan Blok explicitly naming banks, payment providers and hosting services as sharing responsibility puts payment partners on notice that facilitating unlicensed Dutch traffic is becoming a reputational and legal exposure, not just a merchant category question. The appeal on trust firm vetting duties is the one to watch, because if intermediaries acquire a client due-diligence obligation for gambling structures it changes the cost of standing up offshore brands aimed at EU players. The TikTok targeting of young players and the practice of masquerading as licensed brands also means legitimate operators now have a brand-protection problem sitting alongside their channelisation problem. And the ad-ban debate is now framed with hard numbers, which strengthens the argument that acquisition restrictions on licensees hand share to sites with no RG controls at all.",
    "expanded": "iGaming Business reported on 2 September 2026 that state-backed Nederlandse Loterij had ordered the operators of unauthorised gambling site Skyhills to cease serving Dutch players, following a court ruling in the separate Lalabet case. The operator announced on the Tuesday that it had sent cease-and-desist notices to companies and directors connected with Skyhills across Costa Rica, North Macedonia, the United Kingdom, Curacao, Malta and the Marshall Islands, and confirmed the platform is now inaccessible in the Netherlands. CEO Arjan Blok said: 'Skyhills uses devious advertising, including on TikTok, explicitly targeting Dutch players, especially young people. Players there gamble without any safeguards and face all associated risks. This is unacceptable. It is encouraging to see Skyhills go offline immediately after our initial action.' Skyhills is the third illegal operator pursued after Lalabet and Qbet. The Lalabet case reached a milestone in June 2026 when a Dutch court ruled largely in the lottery operator's favour, finding the operators and directors had acted unlawfully in targeting Dutch players and ordering them to cease activity and provide detailed ownership and shareholder information. The court did not find Curacao-based trust firm DECC and its directors liable, but signalled closer scrutiny of intermediaries; Nederlandse Loterij plans to appeal portions of the decision, arguing trust firms have a duty to vet clients properly, and is investigating the same firm's role in relation to Qbet and Skyhills. The action coincided with the Dutch House of Representatives debating gambling reform. Blok welcomed proposals to expand the Gambling Authority's powers but warned legislation could take years, saying: 'The government must act now through criminal prosecutions by the Public Prosecution Service, alongside measures taken by regulators and licensed operators. Tackling illegal gambling requires joint responsibility from banks, payment providers and hosting services who facilitate these platforms.' The operator also criticised June proposals for a blanket advertising ban on licensed providers, arguing it would push players to unregulated sites. Industry body VNLOK puts Dutch black market revenue above EUR 1bn a year, reaching some 200,000 citizens.",
