    "section": "North America",
    "title": "Colorado fines Fanatics $20,000 after VIP team texted promotions to a self-excluded bettor twice",
    "meta": "SBC Americas | 1 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "The Colorado Limited Gaming Control Commission has settled with Fanatics Sportsbook for $20,000 after a Division of Gaming investigation found the operator's VIP team sent promotional texts to a customer who had self-excluded for five years. The bettor joined the state self-exclusion list on 15 January 2026, received a promotional text on 1 February, and received a second one on 17 February despite Fanatics identifying the problem and issuing VIP staff training on 4 February. Fanatics must now audit its self-exclusion list for comparable contacts between 1 January 2024 and 1 March 2026.",
    "why": "The failure here was not the exclusion list itself but the VIP channel sitting outside the automated suppression logic, which is one of the most common and least discussed gaps in operator CRM architecture. Self-exclusion enforcement usually works cleanly on automated campaigns and then breaks the moment a human account manager sends a one-to-one message from a separate tool. That makes this a platform integration problem: exclusion status has to be a hard gate at the messaging layer, not a filter applied to bulk sends, and the remediation Colorado imposed is effectively a demand for retrospective auditability across two years of outbound contact. It also lands in a state that just banned inactivity push notifications, credit card funding and bonus bet language under SB26-131, so Colorado is fast becoming the strictest configuration profile in the US. Vendors should assume RG suppression will need to cover VIP and manual outreach paths, with logs to prove it.",
    "expanded": "The Colorado Limited Gaming Control Commission discussed the Fanatics settlement at its meeting on Thursday 27 August 2026, with the stipulation and agreement signed by Fanatics on 10 June and subsequently published. The Colorado Division of Gaming found the operator failed to comply with state law on customer outreach: the gambler placed himself on the state self-exclusion list for five years on 15 January 2026; a Fanatics VIP team member texted him a promotional offer on 1 February; Fanatics identified the issue on 4 February, notified VIP leadership and distributed training materials to customer-facing VIP staff; and a VIP team member texted the same self-excluded user another promotional offer on 17 February. Regulators concluded that by sending the two messages Fanatics failed to ensure compliance with its own responsible gaming programme strategy, which last year stated the company 'will not intentionally market to any known Self-Excluded individuals.' Beyond the $20,000 penalty, Fanatics must audit its self-exclusion list to establish whether other self-excluded users were contacted between 1 January 2024 and 1 March 2026 and file a detailed report with the Division, and must enhance training for all VIP staff on regulatory risk awareness and responsible gaming protocols with proof submitted to the Division. Fanatics entered Colorado in December 2023 via its takeover of PointsBet USA's licences. More than 1,200 Colorado residents are on the state self-exclusion register. Governor Jared Polis signed SB26-131 in June, banning credit card funding, capping bettors at six deposits per day and prohibiting marketing terms such as 'bonus bet' and 'no sweat'.",
