    "section": "North America",
    "title": "Canadian securities and investment regulators rule out sports event contracts in joint guidance",
    "meta": "InGame | 31 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "The Canadian Securities Administrators and the Canadian Investment Regulatory Organization issued joint guidance on 27 August 2026 stating they do not support sports event contracts being regulated as derivatives or securities in Canada. The regulators confirmed dealer members may only facilitate trading of a limited set of event contracts cleared through certain US regulated exchanges, specifically those based on economic, environmental or financial indicators. The guidance landed on the fifth anniversary of Parliament decriminalising single-event sports betting and one day before the Ninth Circuit ruled against Kalshi in the US.",
    "why": "Canada has just drawn the line the US courts are still arguing over, and it did so through financial regulators rather than gaming ones, which is the cleaner route because it removes the preemption argument entirely. That protects the economics of Ontario's licensed model and Alberta's new market, where operators pay tax and carry player-protection duties that an offshore-cleared event contract would not. For anyone building product for Canada, the practical read is that sports and entertainment contracts stay off the roadmap while economic and financial indicator contracts remain narrowly permissible, so any exchange-style module needs a market-eligibility layer rather than a blanket switch. The existing binary options prohibition on sub-30-day contracts gives regulators an additional and already-tested enforcement tool.",
    "expanded": "A joint statement from the Canadian Securities Administrators and the Canadian Investment Regulatory Organization, released on Thursday 27 August 2026, set out that Canadian financial regulators do not support treating sports event contracts as derivatives or securities. The timing was pointed: it fell on the fifth anniversary of Parliament decriminalising single-event sports betting in Canada. The regulators acknowledged that certain contracts, defined as 'event contracts, prediction contracts or prediction market contracts', do meet Canada's broad existing definitions of securities or derivatives under securities and derivatives legislation, while noting some may be excluded. An accompanying staff memo recommends banning such contracts and confirms neither agency has authorised the offering of any contracts beyond what they deem permissible. The staff position states: 'Dealer members may only facilitate trading of a limited set of Event Contracts that are traded and cleared through certain U.S. regulated exchanges and clearing houses, namely those that are based on economic, environmental or financial indicators.' Some financial institutions do hold permission to offer prediction products in Canada, but the permissible menu is highly restrictive and excludes sports. Regulators issued a reminder to approved companies in April to stay within the rules. Several Canadian jurisdictions already prohibit short-dated contracts under Multilateral Instrument 91-102, which bars 'any person from advertising, offering, selling or otherwise trading a binary option having a term to maturity of less than 30 days, with or to an individual.' The guidance preceded by one day the Ninth Circuit ruling permitting Nevada to enforce its gaming laws against prediction markets. Ontario opened its competitive market in April 2022 and Alberta launched single-event wagering and online casino in July 2026.",
