    "title": "Lula convenes ministers and civil society on online betting, says decision is two thirds made",
    "meta": "Yogonet Latinoamerica | 3 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva met ministers and sector representatives on 1 September to debate the expansion of online betting, calling it an 'evil for society' and saying he already has 'two thirds of the decision made'. Neither the Secretariat of Prizes and Betting (SPA) nor operator bodies ANJL and IBJR were represented. Officials disclosed that 1.2 million people have requested self-exclusion on the federal platform and that 37 fintechs have been notified over flows linked to illegal betting.",
    "why": "The absence of the SPA and the two main licensed-operator associations from a meeting where proposals ran from advertising curbs to outright prohibition is the signal that should worry anyone with a Brazilian roadmap. A ban debate conducted without the technical regulator in the room means product teams cannot price the risk of features they are currently building, from bonus mechanics to marketing integrations. The 1.2 million self-exclusion figure also raises the operational bar on centralised exclusion-register checks at registration and deposit. And the notification of 37 fintechs pulls payment partners directly into the enforcement perimeter, which changes how cashier and PSP orchestration decisions need to be documented.",
    "expanded": "Lula held the session on Tuesday 1 September 2026, streamed live, bringing together ministers and representatives from several parts of Brazilian society to hear 'what Brazilian society thinks' about online betting. He said the government 'has been debating for some time how to solve the betting problem', described betting as an 'evil for society' and closed by saying he had 'two thirds of the decision made', without specifying whether that means prohibition. Notably absent were the SPA of the Ministry of Finance, the body that regulates and supervises the sector, plus the ANJL and the Instituto Brasileiro de Jogo Responsavel. Casa Civil chief minister Miriam Belchior conceded: 'We have been busy regulating and supervising, but this is not proving enough.' Health Minister Alexandre Padilha framed gambling as a public health issue, citing 20,000 places on a professional training course that were oversubscribed, prompting a second tranche of 20,000, and psychological care capacity rising from around 600 consultations a month to 100,000, with contracting for up to 2 million. Sport Minister Paulo Henrique Cordeiro acknowledged around BRL 500 million (USD 96.6 million) had already reached sport in the first half, but said: 'It is difficult to effectively defend this economic activity in the country.' CNC financial vice-president Leandro Domingues Teixeira called a ban an 'extreme but necessary decision'. Investigations by the Ministry of Finance and Federal Police point to illegal schemes potentially moving up to BRL 50 billion; Apple and Google have been contacted over clandestine apps. The final call rests with the president after internal government debate, with no date set.",
