    "title": "Chile's Subtel orders six ISPs to DNS-block 42 betting sites within 48 hours",
    "meta": "Yogonet Latinoamerica | 2 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Chile's telecoms undersecretariat Subtel has formally instructed Entel, Movistar, Claro, GTD, WOM and VTR to block a list of 42 betting and casino domains supplied by the Superintendencia de Casinos de Juego, using DNS-level blocking within a maximum of 48 hours. The order implements rulings by the Supreme Court and the Santiago Court of Appeals in a protection action brought by Loteria de Concepcion. The list includes Betano, Betsson, Coolbet, Rojabet, Betsala, Stake, 1xbet, Novibet, Betcris, Doradobet and bet7k.",
    "why": "This is the first LatAm blocking regime built as a repeatable pipeline rather than a one-off court order, and the three-step design matters commercially: the complainant supplies the domain list, the SCJ certifies it, Subtel serves the ISPs. Any brand operating into Chile from an offshore licence now faces a rolling process explicitly extended to new domains, subdomains and mirror sites. For platform teams the practical consequence is that Chilean traffic routing, affiliate link estates and app-store presence become live compliance surfaces rather than growth channels. It also sets a template that Peru, Colombia and Ecuador regulators can lift wholesale, since DNS was endorsed by the court as proportionate and network-neutral.",
    "expanded": "Subtel announced on 2 September 2026 that, in compliance with rulings by the Corte Suprema and the Corte de Apelaciones de Santiago, it had served six internet service providers with a list of 42 websites remitted by the Superintendencia de Casinos de Juego, instructing DNS blocking within 48 hours. The measure derives from a protection action brought by Loteria de Concepcion against Entel, Movistar, Claro, GTD, WOM and VTR. According to Subtel's technical report, the appeals court endorsed 'the use of Domain Name System (DNS) blocking as the appropriate alternative that safeguards network neutrality and user privacy, acts proportionately and prevents inadvertent over-blocking of lawful services'. Telecommunications undersecretary Romina Garrido said: 'As Subtel we are strictly complying with the rulings of the courts and the regulatory framework, deploying an efficient and coordinated technical solution that safeguards people's digital security.' She added that the office 'demands that telecommunications companies act with maximum speed'. The mechanism runs in three stages: Loteria de Concepcion or other complainants compile domains, subdomains and mirror sites; the SCJ verifies and legally certifies which correspond to unauthorised informal operators; Subtel then notifies the telcos with the annex of domains. ISPs must certify compliance directly to the Santiago Court of Appeals, under the penalty the court has set, and copy Subtel with the exact date and time of execution. The procedure will be applied successively to new domains and mirror sites validated by the SCJ, with provision to correct misidentification or harm to lawful services.",
