    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "Chilean operator associations say Subtel blocking hit VAT-registered platforms and exceeded the court order",
    "meta": "Yogonet Latinoamerica | 3 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Two Chilean iGaming associations have attacked Subtel's blocking order, saying it 'greatly exceeds' what the courts established. The Asociacion de iGaming Responsable says the original ruling named 12 specific websites while the applied list has grown past thirty, selected via 'an irregular mechanism not contemplated in the rules'. aPAL notes the blocks land one month before the first Digital VAT payment by at least 25 betting platforms.",
    "why": "The sharpest detail here is the sequencing: operators that voluntarily registered for Chilean digital VAT are being DNS-blocked a month before their first payment falls due. That is a textbook case of enforcement outrunning licensing and it directly undermines the commercial argument for early voluntary compliance in a pre-regulation market. For a B2B supplier weighing whether to service Chile-facing brands, it means tax registration confers no safe harbour, so contractual protections and geo-controls have to be built assuming both blocking and taxation apply at once. The associations' complaint that responsible operators are penalised while informal ones continue also tests the black-market displacement thesis in real time.",
    "expanded": "Publishing on 3 September 2026, Yogonet reported that aPAL, the Agrupacion Chilena de Plataformas de Apuestas en Linea, whose members include Coolbet, Betsson, Juegaenlinea and Latamwin, said Subtel's decision 'greatly exceeds' the courts' ruling 'by including in the blocking domains and operators that were not part of the original protection action nor are covered by the ruling'. aPAL highlighted the timing, noting the blocking arrives 'one month before the first Digital VAT payment is made by at least 25 betting platforms', and called on the executive and legislature to 'urgently advance the processing of the bill regulating the development of online betting platforms'. The Asociacion de iGaming Responsable, whose members include Betano, Betsala, Estelarbet, Juegalo and Novibet, said 'the application of the measure has exceeded its legal basis', arguing the original ruling identified 12 specific websites while more than thirty are now covered, with extra sites chosen through 'an irregular mechanism not contemplated in the norms that regulate Subtel's powers'. It described 'contradictory signals', pointing out members 'support regulation and have registered to pay digital VAT' yet 'it is their websites that are being blocked'. The association added: 'This punishes responsible operators who are actively seeking to comply with the rules and leaves the field clear for informal operators whose priority is neither responsible gambling nor the protection of minors and vulnerable groups', concluding that 'blocking does not achieve what proper regulation would'. Both bodies argue a comprehensive online gambling framework is the correct route for protecting minors, safeguarding user funds and guaranteeing personal data security. The first digital VAT payment falls due around early October 2026.",
