    "section": "LatAm",
    "title": "LOTBA becomes first Argentine gambling body to clear an independent external AML review",
    "meta": "Yogonet Latinoamerica | 3 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Loteria de la Ciudad de Buenos Aires (LOTBA) has completed the first Independent External Review of its anti-money-laundering and counter-terrorist-financing system, aligning it with the requirements of Argentina's Unidad de Informacion Financiera. The review was carried out by specialist Dr Francisco D'Albora, coordinated with compliance officer and LOTBA director Dr Ezequiel Dominguez and prevention unit head Dra Carolina Galtieri. LOTBA, chaired by Jesus Acevedo, says it is the first gambling body to pass such an audit.",
    "why": "Argentina's provincial regulators each set their own AML expectations, and LOTBA setting an externally audited benchmark in the City of Buenos Aires quietly raises the compliance floor for every licensee and supplier plugged into that jurisdiction. An independent review of a prevention system means the regulator now has documented expectations for transaction monitoring, suspicious activity escalation and record-keeping that it can push down into operator obligations. For PAM and payments vendors, that translates into concrete demands: auditable AML rule sets, exportable evidence trails and role-separated compliance workflows rather than generic reporting. Expect other provinces to cite this as precedent when writing their own supervision programmes.",
    "expanded": "Yogonet reported on 3 September 2026 that LOTBA, the City of Buenos Aires lottery and gaming body chaired by Jesus Acevedo, has become the first gambling organisation to pass an external anti-money-laundering audit, adapting to the regulatory requirements of the Unidad de Informacion Financiera, Argentina's financial intelligence unit. LOTBA completed the first Revision Externa Independiente of its Sistema de Prevencion de Lavado de Activos y Financiacion del Terrorismo. The external evaluation was conducted by specialist Dr Francisco D'Albora and coordinated with Dr Ezequiel Dominguez, LOTBA's compliance officer and a director of the body, together with Dra Carolina Galtieri, who heads its prevention unit. According to the report, the exercise allowed LOTBA to evaluate, coordinate and strengthen its internal controls and supervision procedures. LOTBA framed the outcome as a reaffirmation of its commitment to legal, safe and transparent gaming in the City of Buenos Aires. The report does not attach figures, percentages or deadlines to the review, and no direct quotes were published. The wider context is Argentina's fragmented model, in which each province and the autonomous city license and supervise online gambling separately, meaning AML expectations are set jurisdiction by jurisdiction rather than nationally. An externally validated prevention system in the country's most commercially significant online market gives other provincial regulators a reference point, and gives licensed operators in CABA a clearer sense of the documentary standard their own programmes will be measured against.",
