    "meta": "Focus Gaming News | 1 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Analytics platform Blask has ranked three African markets among the world's top 10 for offshore gaming demand. Offshore operators account for 98.2% of Mali's online gaming market, fourth globally, with Morocco on 64.1% in fifth and Gabon on 40% in ninth. The Blask Index measures online player demand through digital signals such as search behaviour rather than revenue or amounts wagered, comparing interest in locally licensed brands against offshore brands serving players without a local licence.",
    "why": "A 98.2% offshore share in Mali is not a leakage problem, it is the market, and it reframes what entering these countries means for a platform business. Where demand already sits with unlicensed international brands, the commercial prize for a first regulated entrant is not incremental share but the ability to convert existing search intent, which puts brand recognition, local payment rails and Francophone localisation ahead of content depth. Morocco's mix of a 30% withholding tax on offshore winnings and a suspended blocking order shows how partial enforcement leaves offshore brands accessible while taxing their players, which is the worst configuration for anyone trying to build a licensed proposition. Gabon's licence expiry and regularisation cycle is the clearest near-term test of whether a new framework can actually pull demand onshore.",
    "expanded": "Focus Gaming News Africa reported on 1 September 2026 that Blask Index data places Mali, Morocco and Gabon among the ten global markets with the highest offshore share of online gaming demand. Mali ranks fourth worldwide with offshore operators accounting for 98.2% of its online gaming market. Morocco is fifth on 64.1% and Gabon ninth on 40%. The Blask Index does not measure betting revenue or turnover; it uses digital signals including search behaviour to gauge interest in gambling brands, then compares demand between locally licensed operators and offshore brands serving players without a local licence. Mali's regulatory environment is described as still developing, with the country working on a more comprehensive online gambling framework that would include a dedicated regulatory authority and taxation measures. In Morocco, winnings from offshore betting sites have been subject to a 30% withholding tax plus a 2% solidarity contribution since July 2025. A Moroccan court ruling in January 2026 ordered telecoms companies to block a number of offshore betting websites, but an appeals court subsequently suspended that blocking measure, leaving offshore platforms accessible while the legal process continued. Gabon is revising its gambling licensing framework as part of broader regulatory change: existing licences and declarations entered an expiry process in April 2026 and operators were given until 7 July to regularise their positions under the new rules. The publication notes that whether Mali's new rules shift the offshore-heavy picture will depend on implementation and enforcement, and that if Gabon's licensing system is effectively implemented with more operators moving onshore, its offshore share could fall considerably by the next Blask assessment.",
