    "section": "Asia / Oceania",
    "title": "Star Entertainment posts AU$307.3m FY26 loss with going-concern doubt as carded play bites Sydney gaming revenue",
    "meta": "Inside Asian Gaming | 31 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Star Entertainment Group reported a AU$307.3 million (US$220 million) statutory net loss for the year to 30 June 2026, narrowed from AU$428 million a year earlier, with normalised EBITDA loss cut to AU$16.1 million from AU$76 million. Normalised revenue slipped 2.2% to AU$1.1 billion, while group corporate costs fell 38% to AU$178 million. The Star Sydney's gaming revenue dropped 9.1% to AU$499.8 million, which Star attributed to softer table games under mandatory carded play and cash restrictions in New South Wales.",
    "why": "Star is the live experiment in what happens to revenue when account-based play and cash limits are imposed on a large casino, and a 9.1% Sydney gaming decline with EBITDA down 30.6% is the price tag. That number is the most concrete evidence available for anyone arguing about the commercial cost of carded play, and it will be quoted in every jurisdiction debating similar reforms including the NSW pubs and clubs package. The counterpoint sits in the same release: Gold Coast gaming revenue grew 3.2% on machines, and July group revenue was 12% above the March quarter trough, which Star credits to patron experience and marketing work - a retention story rather than an acquisition one. The unresolved AUSTRAC penalty for historical AML failures is the reminder that compliance debt compounds long after the conduct.",
    "expanded": "Star Entertainment Group released FY26 results on Monday 31 August, reporting a AU$307.3 million (US$220 million) statutory net loss for the year to 30 June 2026, an improvement on the prior year's AU$428 million (US$307 million) loss. Normalised EBITDA loss narrowed to AU$16.1 million (US$11.5 million) from AU$76 million. Normalised revenue fell 2.2% to AU$1.1 billion (US$788 million), with the low point in Q3, while a comprehensive review of resourcing and operating model - including closing the corporate office - cut group corporate costs 38% to AU$178 million (US$128 million). By property, The Star Sydney gaming revenue fell 9.1% to AU$499.8 million (US$358 million) with property EBITDA down 30.6% to AU$63.7 million, which Star tied to softer table games under uplifted NSW regulatory controls including mandatory carded play and cash restrictions. The Star Gold Coast grew gaming revenue 3.2% to AU$256.4 million on gaming machine strength, EBITDA up 0.3% to AU$79.8 million. Star booked AU$59.7 million in operator fee revenue from The Star Brisbane and now receives AU$4.5 million monthly from its Hong Kong partners as operator following the sale of its 50% stake. Group CEO Bruce Mathieson Jnr said: 'We have moved to a more accountable, property-led operating model and a renewed focus on performance, customers and responsible operations,' adding that 'returning to suitability remains critical to our future.' July combined Sydney and Gold Coast revenue rose 6% year-on-year to AU$92.4 million. Star acknowledged ongoing material uncertainty over its ability to continue as a going concern, citing a looming AUSTRAC penalty for historical AML failures, delivery of further revenue growth and cost savings, and a return to licence suitability in Sydney. The Gold Coast licence suspension deferral currently runs to 30 September 2026.",
    "source": "https://asgam.com/2026/08/31/star-falls-to-au307-million-loss-in-fy26-insists-worst-is-now-behind-on-long-awaited-revenue-growth-in-july/",
