    "title": "Scott Longley: prediction exchanges are outsourced sportsbook trading rooms, not peer-to-peer markets",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 2 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "In an iGB in-depth piece, Scott Longley reports that professional market-making firms, not retail users, increasingly price the bets and supply the liquidity on US sports prediction exchanges. White Swan Data founder Bernard Marantelli tells him 'Kalshi is a sportsbook that's just not allowed to have an in-house risk team', and says his firm accounts for as much as 40% of activity on some secondary exchanges. Longley's conclusion is that the emerging ecosystem looks less like millions of customers trading predictions and more like an outsourced sportsbook trading room.",
    "source": "https://igamingbusiness.com/prediction-markets/meet-sportsboo-white-swan-prediction-market/",
    "tags": [
      "Prediction markets",
      "Trading and risk",
      "Liquidity",
      "Analysis"
    ],
    "commentary": "Longley's argument is that the peer-to-peer framing of prediction exchanges is a customer-facing story that the market structure does not support. The exchange supplies an API; specialist firms compete to quote prices and commit capital. Marantelli puts it bluntly: 'Here's an API. Bernard and 88 other people can market-make all these request-for-quotes (RFQs). Some people might come in and just do esports because they're esports experts... But it's a sportsbook.' London-based White Swan concentrates on RFQ parlay markets for the reason any trading business would: 'Just better margins... It's the area that fewer people can do well. So I think it's more defendable margin, more ability to get long-term contracts.' Marantelli names White Swan and Susquehanna as operating at industrial scale in parlays, with Jump Trading, Mojo and DL Trading in a possible leading group, plus smaller syndicates running USD 5m to USD 10m. Veltium CEO Enda Kendrick adds that the largest UK and European sharp-betting groups have moved rapidly into the US and that more than 100 smaller operations want in, noting two ordinary customers are not going to put USD 10m or USD 20m behind the Philadelphia Eagles. The sharpest part of Longley's piece is the harm and sustainability angle. 'People will lose money faster on exchanges for lots of reasons,' Marantelli says. 'It inherently increases spend, volatility, lots of things. And you're playing against a sharper audience than you're playing against at the DraftKings sportsbook.' Kendrick's warning from exchange history is a poker table where, once the weak players leave, the fourth-best professional becomes a loser. White Swan expects NFL prediction markets could generate USD 5bn to USD 7bn of liability in a single week, and Marantelli says Kalshi grew clients fivefold during the World Cup. As he concludes: 'Let's call a spade a spade.'",
    "takeaway": "If exchanges are functionally sportsbooks with the risk function outsourced, product teams should treat them as a trading and liquidity problem, not a novel category. Two concrete implications. First, RFQ and correlated-parlay pricing is where defensible margin sits, so the capability worth investing in is dynamic correlation modelling and low-latency quote infrastructure, not more market breadth. Second, Marantelli's point that customers lose faster on exchanges is a responsible gambling and lifetime-value warning with direct platform consequences: position-holding and re-entry behaviour needs its own RG signal set, because existing models tuned to stake size and session length will not catch a user refusing to close a losing position. Anyone building order-book or exchange-style products should also assume the counterparty is professional and price and monitor accordingly."
